{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q001_histone_related_proteins","answer":"In current SCOPe release 2.08 (latest periodic update 2.08-2026-08-16), the closest classified group is the all-alpha Histone-fold superfamily a.22.1 (SUNID 47113), for which SCOPe explicitly indicates superfamily-level homology. Within it, H2A, H2B, H3, H4, and the macro-H2A.1 histone domain share the Nucleosome core histones family a.22.1.1 (SUNID 47114), the strongest listed relationship. Other homologous but more remote families are archaeal histones a.22.1.2 (SUNID 47129); the TAF-associated-factor group a.22.1.3 (SUNID 47134), including TAF histone-fold domains, CHRAC-14/16, NC2 alpha/beta, NF-YB/NF-YC, and the Son-of-sevenless histone domain; and bacterial histone-fold proteins a.22.1.4 (SUNID 101318), including Aq_328 and TTHA1479. These families share superfamily a.22.1 and fold a.22, but family separation denotes greater evolutionary and functional divergence: a histone-fold domain in a TAF or NF-Y subunit does not justify transferring nucleosome-packaging function, substrate specificity, oligomeric state, or whole-protein homology. Domain matches in multidomain proteins apply only to the classified domain.

Names alone can be misleading. Linker histones H1/H5 belong to family a.4.5.13 in the winged-helix superfamily a.4.5 and DNA/RNA-binding three-helical-bundle fold a.4, so they share only the broad all-alpha class with core histones, not the histone fold. H-NS “histone-like” proteins are in a separate fold/superfamily, a.155/a.155.1; their histone-like label reflects chromatin-associated behavior rather than close SCOPe homology to a.22.1. Histone deacetylases, methyltransferases, and acyltransferases are histone-modifying enzymes rather than histone relatives; for example, HDAC8 is c.42.1.2 in the alpha/beta arginase/deacetylase fold. The separately classified H3 residues 130–135 entry j.63.1.1 is a peptide fragment, not another full-length histone-fold protein.

For inference or template selection, prefer a representative from the same family, with comparable domain boundaries and biological assembly. Superfamily membership supports remote homology but not automatic function transfer; fold membership alone normally supports structural resemblance rather than common ancestry, and class membership is weaker still. The a.22.1.0 “automated matches” grouping should not be treated as a distinct curated biological family or as a preferred representative without independent validation.","evidence_used":["Pretrained model knowledge","/api/v1/","/api/v1/openapi.yaml","/api/v1/releases/2.08-2026-08-16/search?q=histone&mode=token&limit=500","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47113","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47113/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47113/children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47113/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47112/children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47114/children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47129/children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47134/children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/101318/children","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/levels","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/46828/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81272/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/110587/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/58715/parents"]}]}
